  <!-- ── Is Travel Insurance Enough? ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Is Travel Insurance Enough to Replace Health Insurance for the NLV?</h2>
        <p>Short answer: No. Here's why.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--red">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⛔</span>
        <p><strong>Critical requirement:</strong> The Spanish Non-Lucrative Visa requires mandatory health insurance that is <strong>continuous, ongoing, and residential</strong> — covering routine and emergency care with no co-payment and no exclusions for pre-existing conditions. Travel insurance cannot meet this requirement.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__feature-grid">
        <div class="msv__feature-item">
          <span class="msv__feature-icon">❌</span>
          <div>
            <h4>Travel Insurance is Temporary</h4>
            <p>Travel insurance typically covers a specific trip (1–30 days) or annual travel (multiple trips up to 365 days total). It's not continuous residential coverage required by the NLV.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__feature-item">
          <span class="msv__feature-icon">❌</span>
          <div>
            <h4>Limited Scope for Routine Care</h4>
            <p>Travel insurance covers emergencies abroad, not routine doctor visits, prescriptions, or planned treatments — all essential needs as a Spanish resident that your health insurance must cover.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__feature-item">
          <span class="msv__feature-icon">❌</span>
          <div>
            <h4>Pre-existing Conditions Often Excluded</h4>
            <p>Travel insurance frequently excludes or limits coverage for pre-existing conditions. The NLV requires health insurance with full coverage and no such exclusions.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__feature-item">
          <span class="msv__feature-icon">❌</span>
          <div>
            <h4>Visa Applications Won't Accept It</h4>
            <p>The Spanish immigration authority requires a formal health insurance policy as proof. Travel insurance is not recognized as meeting this requirement — your application will be rejected.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">The Right Approach:</h3>
      <ul style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <li><strong>For your NLV:</strong> Get a proper residential health insurance policy (€50–150/month) that meets visa requirements — no co-payment, comprehensive coverage.</li>
        <li><strong>As a supplement:</strong> Add travel insurance (€30–80/month for annual multi-trip) for extra protection during travel and emergencies abroad.</li>
        <li><strong>For the transition:</strong> Use travel insurance to cover the gap between arrival and when your residential insurance activates.</li>
      </ul>

      <p style="color: var(--muted); font-style: italic;">You need both types of insurance working together — not one replacing the other.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

