  <!-- ── Repatriation & Emergency Coverage ──────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Repatriation & Emergency Medical Evacuation</h2>
        <p>One of the most valuable aspects of travel insurance is repatriation coverage — here's what it means and why it matters.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">What is Repatriation Coverage?</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Repatriation coverage pays for you to be medically transported back to your home country or to the nearest appropriate medical facility if you suffer a serious medical emergency while traveling that cannot be treated in Spain or the destination country. This includes:</p>

      <ul style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <li><strong>Emergency air transport:</strong> If you need a medical airplane, helicopter, or other emergency transport home</li>
        <li><strong>Medical escort:</strong> A doctor or nurse travels with you if needed</li>
        <li><strong>Hospital-to-hospital transfer:</strong> Moving you from a rural or under-equipped hospital to a major medical center</li>
        <li><strong>Medication & supplies:</strong> Emergency medicines and medical equipment for transport</li>
        <li><strong>Family accompaniment:</strong> Sometimes covers a family member traveling with you</li>
        <li><strong>Post-repatriation care:</strong> Some policies continue covering treatment after you arrive home</li>
      </ul>

      <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1.5rem; margin: 2rem 0;">
        <div style="background: #f0fdf4; border-left: 4px solid #22c55e; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
          <h4 style="color: var(--navy); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">When Repatriation is Covered</h4>
          <ul style="margin: 0;">
            <li>Serious injury or illness requiring advanced treatment unavailable locally</li>
            <li>Life-threatening medical conditions needing specialist care</li>
            <li>Complications from surgery or emergency treatment</li>
            <li>Psychological breakdown or serious mental health crisis</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div style="background: #fef2f2; border-left: 4px solid var(--red); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
          <h4 style="color: var(--navy); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Common Exclusions</h4>
          <ul style="margin: 0;">
            <li>Repatriation for non-medical reasons (homesickness, relationship issues)</li>
            <li>Pre-existing conditions (often excluded; check policy)</li>
            <li>Travel against medical advice</li>
            <li>High-risk activities not declared to insurer</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>Cost note:</strong> Emergency medical evacuation can cost €100,000+ if not insured. A recent case of a tourist needing helicopter rescue in the Alps cost €200,000. Travel insurance repatriation coverage is one of the best investments an expat can make.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Typical Repatriation Coverage Limits</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Budget policies (€20–30/month):</strong> €500,000–€1,000,000 repatriation limit</li>
        <li><strong>Standard policies (€40–60/month):</strong> €1,000,000–€3,000,000 repatriation limit</li>
        <li><strong>Premium policies (€70–100+/month):</strong> €5,000,000+ repatriation limit, higher emergency medical cover</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

