  <!-- ── FAQs ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section" id="faq">
    <div class="msv__container">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p>Get answers to the most common questions about travel insurance for NLV holders.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>Is travel insurance required for the Non-Lucrative Visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>No — travel insurance is not a visa requirement. However, <strong>health insurance is mandatory</strong> for the NLV (€50–150/month). Travel insurance is recommended as a supplementary policy, especially for your initial arrival, Schengen travel, and emergency medical evacuation. Think of it as an add-on layer of protection, not a visa requirement.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What's the difference between travel insurance and health insurance?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p><strong>Health insurance</strong> covers routine and emergency medical care in Spain — doctor visits, prescriptions, hospital stays. It's required for the NLV and is ongoing, continuous coverage.</p>
            <p><strong>Travel insurance</strong> is temporary coverage (1–365 days) that covers emergencies while traveling, trip cancellation, baggage loss, and repatriation. It does <em>not</em> cover routine healthcare and cannot replace health insurance for visa purposes.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Can I use my UK EHIC or GHIC card as travel insurance?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>No. The EHIC (European Health Insurance Card) and UK GHIC are designed for temporary travelers visiting other countries, not permanent residents. Once you become a legal resident in Spain, these cards are no longer valid. You must hold Spanish residential health insurance plus travel insurance for trips outside Spain. Check your card's terms — it typically says "not valid for persons taking up residence."</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Do I need travel insurance for my initial arrival in Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes, you should have travel insurance from the moment you book your flight. Your residential health insurance may not be active until after you've registered with Spanish authorities and received your NIE. Travel insurance bridges the gap between arrival and when your health insurance activates — typically the first 30–90 days. After that, it becomes supplementary for trips outside Spain.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What does repatriation coverage mean and why is it important?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Repatriation coverage pays for you to be medically transported back to your home country or nearest appropriate facility if you suffer a serious medical emergency abroad that can't be treated locally. This includes emergency air transport, medical escorts, and related costs. For NLV holders, this is crucial because emergency evacuation can cost €100,000+. It's one of the most valuable parts of travel insurance.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How much does travel insurance cost for NLV holders?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p><strong>Single-trip policies:</strong> €15–40 per trip (1–31 days)</p>
            <p><strong>Multi-trip annual policies:</strong> €30–80 per year (unlimited trips up to 365 days)</p>
            <p><strong>Resident travel insurance:</strong> €50–120 per year (higher limits, better expat coverage)</p>
            <p>For most NLV holders, a multi-trip annual policy (€50–70/year) is the most cost-effective option, especially if you travel more than 2–3 times per year.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Will travel insurance cover me during my NLV visa renewal?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>You <em>should not</em> travel during visa renewal processing. However, if you do travel while your renewal is pending, travel insurance will cover you. Keep your travel insurance continuous while your renewal application is being reviewed, just as a safety precaution.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What medical cover limit do I need for Schengen travel?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>While not strictly required for EU/EEA citizens like NLV holders, Schengen countries typically encourage (and some technically require) proof of medical cover of at least €30,000–€100,000. Most travel insurance policies offer €250,000+ medical emergency cover, which far exceeds these minimums. Always aim for at least €250,000 in medical cover for peace of mind.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

